  "query_preview": "..Overall benign conditions are expected across the\nregion today. Some isolated rain showers are ongoing early this\nafternoon mainly across portions of southeast Utah, courtesy of a\nmodest influx of moisture associated with a weakening cutoff low \nlifting through the Four Corners region today into Monday. A bit \nmore instability is expected across the region on Monday, which\nwill favor a bit more organized storms capable of producing\nlightning, especially the higher terrain across southern and\ncentral Utah. Similar conditions are expected Tuesday, though\npotential for afternoon showers and thunderstorms will extend\nfurther north into northern Utah and southwest Wyoming.\n\nA more active pattern is expected mid to late week as a trough\ndeepening off the west coast ejects into the southern Great Basin\nthrough this period. Southerly flow increasing ahead of this\nsystem will yield enhanced south to southwest winds across the\nregion by Wednesday afternoon and continue into Thursday. \nStrongest winds are anticipated across southwest Utah late\nWednesday and Thursday afternoon, with widespread gusts 30-35 mph.\nAn efficient tap of tropical moisture will accompany this system,\nwith deep moisture overspreading southern Utah and resulting in\nincreasing coverage of widespread precipitation capable of \nproducing heavy rainfall across the area as early as Wednesday \nafternoon. Precipitation will continue to spread northward \novernight into Thursday, becoming widespread across all of Utah \nand southwest Wyoming through the day. \n\nPrecipitation coverage will favor northern Utah and southwest \nWyoming on Friday as the aforementioned system phases with a \nnorthern stream trough, consolidating much of the deep moisture\nacross this area. As such, coverage of showers and storms will\ntaper off across central and southern Utah gradually through\nFriday morning. While snow levels will hover around 10500-11000\nfeet through most of Wednesday and Thursday, this northern stream\ntrough will usher in a cooler airmass on Friday, allowing snow\nlevels to drop to around 8000 feet by late Friday night. As such,\nmodest snow accumulations are expected across the high terrain of\nnorthern Utah, with 3-6\" expected generally above 9500 feet.\n\nThis extended period of rainfall between Wednesday and Friday will\nlikely be beneficial rain for many areas that aids our soil \nmoistures before heading into winter. Flooding will still be a\nthreat, especially for sensitive terrain across central and\nsouthern Utah, given the potential for an extended period of \nmoderate to heavy rainfall. Models show quite a bit of spread in \nprecipitation accumulations between this period. Across central\nand southern Utah, there is a 75% chance for precipitation\naccumulations to exceed 0.5-1.0\", and a 25% chance to exceed\n1.0-2.0\". Across northern Utah, there is a 75% chance for\nprecipitation accumulations to exceed 0.2-0.6\", and a 25% chance\nto exceed 0.6-1.2\". Heaviest precipitation is favored for the\nsouthern mountains, where there is a 10% chance for accumulations\nclose to 3\".",
